# Example environment for apps/web: the variables the app needs, never their
# real values. Variables are added here as features need them, grouped by the
# service they belong to, each with where its value comes from.
# Write each line as NAME=value, with no spaces around the =.
#
# Locally: copy this file to .env.local in this folder and fill in the
# values. .env.local is gitignored; never commit real keys or secrets.
# On Vercel: Vercel Dashboard → the project → Settings → Environment
# Variables → Add; pick Preview and Production as each variable says, and
# mark the secret ones Sensitive. A VITE_ variable can't be marked secret: it
# reaches the browser.
#
# Server code reads any variable from process.env, inside handlers and
# middleware rather than at module scope. Only variables prefixed with VITE_
# reach the browser (import.meta.env), so a secret must never get that prefix.
# The server checks every Required one at start and names what's missing
# (src/lib/env.server.ts, same groups and order as here).
#
# CI's secrets for applying migrations (GitHub, not this file) are listed in
# packages/database/README.md, "Deploying the schema".

# --- Identity: Clerk (spec 006) ----------------------------------------------
# One Clerk application, "Cartograph". Its development instance's keys are
# used locally, on Preview, and on Production too until the project has a
# domain of its own (spec 006 plan D-10); then Production takes the
# production instance's (pk_live_, sk_live_).

# Required. Public: identifies the Clerk instance to the browser.
#   Local and Vercel: Clerk Dashboard → Cartograph → Configure → API keys →
#   Publishable key (pk_test_…). Vercel: Preview and Production.
VITE_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Required. Secret: server only, for Clerk's Backend API (invitations).
#   Local and Vercel: Clerk Dashboard → Cartograph → Configure → API keys →
#   Secret keys → copy (sk_test_…). Vercel: Preview and Production, Sensitive.
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Required. Secret: proves a webhook notice is Clerk's (spec 007, plan D-07).
#   Vercel: Clerk Dashboard → Cartograph → Configure → Webhooks → Add
#   Endpoint; Endpoint URL https://<production domain>/api/webhooks/clerk,
#   event organization.deleted only → Create; then on the endpoint's page,
#   Signing Secret → reveal → copy (whsec_ and about 44 letters, digits, +,
#   /, =). Preview and Production, Sensitive. To check it later: the
#   endpoint's Testing tab → send organization.deleted → Message attempts
#   shows 204 (400 means this value doesn't match the endpoint's).
#   Local: Clerk can't reach your machine, so any well-formed value starts
#   the app: node -e "console.log('whsec_' + require('crypto').randomBytes(24).toString('base64'))"
#   For real notices locally: npx clerk webhooks listen --forward-to
#   http://localhost:3000/api/webhooks/clerk
CLERK_WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET=whsec_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# --- Storage: Supabase (spec 006; tables from 007) ---------------------------
# Locally, always the local database, never a deployed project: its data
# isn't for development, and sample data lives only locally (spec 007, C-04,
# plan D-05). Start it with `pnpm --filter @workspace/database db:start`;
# `pnpm --filter @workspace/database exec supabase status` prints its three
# values again (Project URL, Publishable key, Secret key).
# On Vercel, Preview and Production both take the one cloud project's values
# until a staging project exists (plan D-05).

# Required. Public: the database's API address, read by the server and by the
# browser, which hears a run's stages from it (spec 008, plan D-10).
#   Local: http://127.0.0.1:54321 (supabase status → Project URL).
#   Vercel: Supabase Dashboard → the project → Project Settings → Data API →
#   Project URL (https://<project ref>.supabase.co). Preview and Production.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321

# Required. Public: safe to expose, as row-level policies guard every row and
# every channel; read by the server and by the browser (plan D-10).
#   Local: supabase status → Publishable key.
#   Vercel: Supabase Dashboard → the project → Project Settings → API Keys →
#   Publishable key (sb_publishable_…). Preview and Production.
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Required. Secret: server only, for the requests no person makes: the Clerk
# webhook deleting a deleted organization's rows (spec 007, plan D-07) and a
# run's writes after the response (spec 008, plan D-06). It bypasses
# row-level access, so a deployed project's never goes in .env.local.
#   Local: supabase status → Secret key (the local database's own).
#   Vercel: Supabase Dashboard → the project → Project Settings → API Keys →
#   Secret keys → Create new secret key, named server, so it can be revoked
#   alone → reveal → copy (sb_secret_…). Preview and Production, Sensitive.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# --- AI: the model and its tracing (spec 009) --------------------------------
# Both on free plans, $0 in all (spec 009 plan): never add billing to either.

# Required. Secret: the key for Gemini, the model that writes explanations.
#   Google AI Studio → Get API key (https://aistudio.google.com/apikey) →
#   Create API key → copy. Keep its project on the free tier: don't set up
#   billing, so it can't be charged (at the limit it answers "try again").
#   Local and Vercel (Preview and Production, Sensitive) use the same key.
GEMINI_API_KEY=your-gemini-api-key

# Optional. "true" records every model call in LangSmith (spec 009, FR-011);
# unset or "false", requests still work and the interface says tracing is
# off (FR-013).
#   LangSmith's free Developer plan: 5,000 traces a month, kept 14 days. A
#   personal organization is capped there until a card is added: add none,
#   and it can't charge.
LANGSMITH_TRACING=true

# Optional; required when LANGSMITH_TRACING is true. Secret.
#   LangSmith → Settings → API Keys → "+ API Key" → Description: cartograph
#   → Key Type: "Tracing Only", the third card ("Org-owned, write-only keys
#   for agents"): it can send traces and nothing else → Expiration: 1 year →
#   Create API Key → copy at once: it's shown only once (lsv2_tr…). The one
#   key serves local and Vercel (Preview and Production, Sensitive).
LANGSMITH_API_KEY=lsv2_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Optional. The account's region; unset means the US. This account is in
# APAC (the address bar shows apac.smith.langchain.com):
#   EU: https://eu.api.smith.langchain.com
#   APAC: https://apac.api.smith.langchain.com
LANGSMITH_ENDPOINT=https://apac.api.smith.langchain.com

# Optional. Where traces land; LangSmith creates it with the first trace.
# One per environment, so local runs never mix with production's:
#   Local: cartograph-development. Vercel: cartograph-preview (Preview),
#   cartograph-production (Production).
LANGSMITH_PROJECT=cartograph-development

# --- Hosting: Vercel ---------------------------------------------------------

# Optional. Set by Vercel on every build: production's domain, for search and
# share tags (src/lib/site.ts). A Vercel build without it stops.
#   Vercel: nothing to do; it's a system variable (Settings → Environment
#   Variables → "Automatically expose System Environment Variables" on).
# VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL=cartograph.example.com

# --- Tools -------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional. The Chromium browser scripts/og-image.ts draws the share image
# with; Edge or Chrome is found without it.
# BROWSER=/usr/bin/chromium
